import { PrincipleCopy } from "../principle"

pub fn copy -> PrincipleCopy = PrincipleCopy {
  title: "Backend-independent semantics",
  summary: "Seseragi defines what your code means. The language used to implement or run it does not quietly change its results.",
  meaningTitle: "The generated language does not define the rules",
  meaningBody: "A compiler backend turns Seseragi into code that a runtime can execute. The current backend emits TypeScript, but TypeScript and JavaScript rules are not automatically Seseragi rules. The backend must implement Seseragi's specified behavior.",
  exampleTitle: "Integer division is different from floating-point division",
  readingTitle: "How to read the example",
  reading: [
    "integerResult is an Int. Dividing -7 by 2 discards the fractional part toward zero, giving -3, not -3.5 or -4. decimalResult uses Float operands and gives 3.5.",
    "The program prints -3, 3.5. JavaScript's ordinary division would give -3.5 for -7 / 2, so simply translating the operator without preserving its Int behavior would be incorrect. An implementation must retain the distinction."
  ],
  reasonTitle: "What you can rely on",
  reason: [
    "You read an integer calculation using the integer rules, regardless of how generated code represents the value. The same requirement applies to left-to-right, once-only argument evaluation and immutable records.",
    "Effects must also remain delayed until execution. A compiler cannot run an Effect early, change the order of visible operations, or skip resource cleanup just because that would be convenient in its output language. These are language guarantees, not accidental behavior of one host."
  ],
  boundaryTitle: "Equal meaning does not promise equal speed or available services",
  boundary: [
    "This principle does not claim that multiple production backends already exist, or that every program can run in every environment. The current implementation uses a TypeScript runtime. A browser and a process also provide different services, so code requiring a file service needs an appropriate host.",
    "Implementations may choose different data layouts and optimizations, and their speed or memory use may differ. Values from foreign code still need explicit conversion into Seseragi types; backend independence does not make arbitrary external values safe."
  ],
}
